  <Document>
    <name>Sample 20 Precise Water Installations (for Review)</name>
    <description>20 randomly sampled archaeological water installation sites with verified precise coordinates from Dr. Zvika Tzuk's dissertation.</description>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</name>
      <description>A large, complex water system was constructed in the 10th-9th century BCE (Iron Age IIA) in the southwestern corner of the tel. It consists of an entrance, a deep shaft (approx. 30m), and a 25m long tunnel leading to the underground water table, designed to secure water supply during sieges. Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. A well was found at Tel Hazor, dated to the Iron Age II period. Cisterns or pools were possibly identified at Tel Hazor during the Iron Age II, though their identification is noted with some uncertainty. A major water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Tel Hazor, dating to the Iron Age II.&#10;&#10;Installation: Water Tunnel Shaft&#10;Era: 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)&#10;Region: Upper Galilee&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.9.1, 2.9.51&#10;Pages: 119, 120, 121, 185, 187, 191</description>
      <Style>
        <IconStyle>
          <color>ff0000ff</color>
          <scale>1.2</scale>
          <Icon>
            <href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href>
          </Icon>
        </IconStyle>
      </Style>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Sample_Number"><value>1</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל חצור</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Upper Galilee</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.1, 2.9.51</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>119, 120, 121, 185, 187, 191</value></Data>
        <Data name="Summary"><value>A large, complex water system was constructed in the 10th-9th century BCE (Iron Age IIA) in the southwestern corner of the tel. It consists of an entrance, a deep shaft (approx. 30m), and a 25m long tunnel leading to the underground water table, designed to secure water supply during sieges. Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. A well was found at Tel Hazor, dated to the Iron Age II period. Cisterns or pools were possibly identified at Tel Hazor during the Iron Age II, though their identification is noted with some uncertainty. A major water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Tel Hazor, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.5681,33.0175,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
