    <Placemark>
      <name>Khirbet el-Khuwwakh (Etam) (חורבת אל-ח&apos;וחי (עיטם))</name>
      <description>A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Ayyats during the Iron Age II. A large natural karstic cave, 42m long, 4m wide, and 2m high, with an estimated capacity of 300 cubic meters. It was plastered with two layers of plaster dating to Iron Age II, indicating its use as a water reservoir, with an entrance for drawing water and another for collecting runoff or spring water. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city.&#10;&#10;Installation: Water Reservoir&#10;Era: 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)&#10;Region: Judean Hills&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.9.11, Summary (186)&#10;Pages: 151, 186, 191</description>
      <Style>
        <IconStyle>
          <color>ff0000ff</color>
          <scale>1.2</scale>
          <Icon>
            <href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href>
          </Icon>
        </IconStyle>
      </Style>
