        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.11, Summary (186)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>151, 186, 191</value></Data>
        <Data name="Summary"><value>A reservoir with an external feeding system was identified at Ayyats during the Iron Age II. A large natural karstic cave, 42m long, 4m wide, and 2m high, with an estimated capacity of 300 cubic meters. It was plastered with two layers of plaster dating to Iron Age II, indicating its use as a water reservoir, with an entrance for drawing water and another for collecting runoff or spring water. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.178,31.6885,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Abu Huf (אבו חוף)</name>
      <description>A round well, 1.5-1.55m in inner diameter and 2.45m deep, constructed of dry-laid fieldstones. It was dug into gravel alluvium next to a wadi channel and was associated with a building and courtyard.&#10;&#10;Installation: Deep Well&#10;Era: 04. Chalcolithic Period (-4500 to -3300 BCE)&#10;Region: Shephelah&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.4.1&#10;Pages: 52, 54</description>
