    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Hazor (תל חצור)</name>
      <description>A large underground water reservoir (No. 357) in the Upper City, within the Late Bronze Age palace area (Stratum XIV). It features a built entrance corridor with a corbelled arch and a rock-cut section extending 30m to a depth of 8.5m, ending in a cruciform shape. It was plastered and had carved steps, with a capacity of about 150 cubic meters. Initially, it might have been a burial cave (Stratum XV) before being converted to a reservoir. A bottle-shaped cistern, dated to the Late Bronze Age, found in the western part of the Iron Age water system entrance. Its neck was destroyed during the Iron Age construction, but it contained many intact Late Bronze Age vessels.&#10;&#10;Installation: Water Reservoir&#10;Era: 07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)&#10;Region: Upper Galilee&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.7.5&#10;Pages: 101, 102</description>
      <Style>
        <IconStyle>
          <color>ff0000ff</color>
          <scale>1.2</scale>
          <Icon>
            <href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href>
          </Icon>
        </IconStyle>
      </Style>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Sample_Number"><value>8</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>07. Late Bronze Age (LB) (-1550 to -1200 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Reservoir</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל חצור</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Upper Galilee</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1550 to -1200 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.7.5</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>101, 102</value></Data>
        <Data name="Summary"><value>A large underground water reservoir (No. 357) in the Upper City, within the Late Bronze Age palace area (Stratum XIV). It features a built entrance corridor with a corbelled arch and a rock-cut section extending 30m to a depth of 8.5m, ending in a cruciform shape. It was plastered and had carved steps, with a capacity of about 150 cubic meters. Initially, it might have been a burial cave (Stratum XV) before being converted to a reservoir. A bottle-shaped cistern, dated to the Late Bronze Age, found in the western part of the Iron Age water system entrance. Its neck was destroyed during the Iron Age construction, but it contained many intact Late Bronze Age vessels.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.5681,33.0275,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Kfar Samir (כפר סמיר)</name>
      <description>A rectangular pit (1x0.8m) dug to 2m depth, constructed with alternating layers of stone and oak wood, widening at the bottom. It is identified as a well, unique in its construction method, and possibly used for capturing fresh water springs.&#10;&#10;Installation: Deep Well&#10;Era: 03. Pottery Neolithic (PN) (-5500 to -4500 BCE)&#10;Region: Coastal Plain&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.3.1.א&#10;Pages: 49, 51</description>
