        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>חורבת טוב</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.16, Summary (186)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>158, 186, 187, 191</value></Data>
        <Data name="Summary"><value>A fortress from the 7th century BCE with a water system comprising a well (3.9m diameter and depth) located 46m from the fortress, and a channel passing through the fortress wall, likely for collecting runoff and possibly supplemented by the well. An unlocated water reservoir within the fortress is hypothesized. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with a monumental water system, likely involving a tunnel or shaft to access groundwater within the fortified city. Wells were found at Khirbet Tov, which are dated with high probability to the Iron Age II period. A reservoir with an external feeding system was possibly identified at Khirbet Tov during the Iron Age II, though its identification is noted with some uncertainty.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.105,30.985,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Kfar Galim North (כפר גלים צפון)</name>
      <description>Five circular pits were found, one of which was excavated to 0.7m depth, dug into hard clay and lined with kurkar and limestone. It had an outer diameter of 1.5m and an inner diameter of 0.8-0.9m, identified as a well.&#10;&#10;Installation: Deep Well&#10;Era: 03. Pottery Neolithic (PN) (-5500 to -4500 BCE)&#10;Region: Coastal Plain&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.3.1.ב&#10;Pages: 49</description>
