    <Placemark>
      <name>Gibeon (el-Jib) (גבעון (אל-ג&apos;יב))</name>
      <description>A system comprising a horizontal tunnel from a spring to a collection chamber, and a 52m long stepped tunnel ascending into the fortified city, dated by the author to the 10th or 9th century BCE (early Iron Age II). A large circular stepped shaft, 11.3m in diameter and 24m deep, cut into rock with a spiral staircase, designed to access groundwater. The author dates its construction to the 7th century BCE (late Iron Age II). Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with two monumental water systems, likely involving tunnels or shafts to access groundwater within the fortified city. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Gibeon, dating to the Iron Age II.&#10;&#10;Installation: Water Tunnel Shaft&#10;Era: 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)&#10;Region: Judean Hills&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.9.5, 2.9.51, Summary (186)&#10;Pages: 128, 129, 130, 131, 185, 186, 187, 191</description>
      <Style>
        <IconStyle>
          <color>ff0000ff</color>
          <scale>1.2</scale>
          <Icon>
            <href>https://maps.google.com/mapfiles/kml/paddle/red-circle.png</href>
          </Icon>
        </IconStyle>
      </Style>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Sample_Number"><value>18</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Water Tunnel Shaft</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>גבעון (אל-ג&apos;יב)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Judean Hills</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-1000 to -586 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.9.5, 2.9.51, Summary (186)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>128, 129, 130, 131, 185, 186, 187, 191</value></Data>
        <Data name="Summary"><value>A system comprising a horizontal tunnel from a spring to a collection chamber, and a 52m long stepped tunnel ascending into the fortified city, dated by the author to the 10th or 9th century BCE (early Iron Age II). A large circular stepped shaft, 11.3m in diameter and 24m deep, cut into rock with a spiral staircase, designed to access groundwater. The author dates its construction to the 7th century BCE (late Iron Age II). Mentioned as a site with an underground water system where a pulley could have been used, similar to the one depicted in an Assyrian relief, suggesting a sophisticated water supply during sieges. Listed as one of 17 sites in Iron Age II Israel with two monumental water systems, likely involving tunnels or shafts to access groundwater within the fortified city. A water system, cut to a spring or groundwater within the fortified city, was found at Gibeon, dating to the Iron Age II.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.174,31.859,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Halif (תל חליף)</name>
      <description>A rectangular, well-plastered cistern, measuring 9x4x3.5m, was uncovered in Area IV. It is dated to the Iron Age II period. Cisterns or pools were found at Tel Halif, dated to the Iron Age II period.&#10;&#10;Installation: Plastered Cistern&#10;Era: 09. Iron Age II (-1000 to -586 BCE)&#10;Region: Shephelah&#10;Precision: Exact Archaeological Site / IAA Grid&#10;Section: 2.9.32&#10;Pages: 168, 191</description>
